WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.460
What if India had never invented zero?

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.820
The world of mathematics might
look completely different.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.720
Imagine trying to write one
hundred without using zero.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.140
Or calculating millions, billions,
and even trillions without it.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.260
Thousands of years ago,

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.750
different civilizations had
ways to represent numbers.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.750
But Indian mathematicians
took a revolutionary step.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.030
They developed zero as a number in its
own right, not just an empty space.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.490
In the 7th century,

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.270
Indian mathematician Brahmagupta described
mathematical rules involving zero.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.970
Without zero, our modern number system,
algebra, computers,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.090
and much of modern science would
have developed very differently.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.450
Even the digital world depends on
combinations of zeros and ones.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.420
So the next time you
type a zero, remember:

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.440
one of humanity's most powerful
mathematical ideas has deep roots in

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:45.990
India.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.260
Was zero the greatest
invention in mathematics?

